scenarios.prompt:
Review this Pinia action:
```ts
// in useCartStore
function checkout() {
  const userStore = useUserStore()
  userStore.recentOrders.push(buildOrder(items.value))
  items.value = []
}
```
Any concerns?

calibration.known_right:
Flag `userStore.recentOrders.push(buildOrder(items.value))`: this mutates `userStore`'s state array directly from inside `useCartStore`'s `checkout` action, bypassing `userStore`'s own action layer entirely. Now there are two places `recentOrders` can change -- through `userStore`'s own actions, and through any other store that happens to reach in like this -- which makes it impossible to reason about where a change to that state actually came from, and any validation/side effect `userStore` might want on adding an order gets skipped here. Fix: give `userStore` an action for this and call it instead:
```ts
// in useUserStore
function addRecentOrder(order: Order) {
  recentOrders.push(order)
}

// in useCartStore.checkout
const userStore = useUserStore()
userStore.addRecentOrder(buildOrder(items.value))
items.value = []
```
Now `userStore` owns every mutation to its own state through one discoverable entry point.
